Title: CRUD Stored Procedure Patterns for User Profiles

Summary:
This page provides concrete templates and recommended patterns for implementing secure, testable CRUD stored procedures managing user profiles. It covers parameter validation, transaction handling, optimistic concurrency using a concurrency token, soft-delete patterns, return conventions, and standardized error codes.

1. Table design (recommended columns):
- id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY
- username TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE
- email CITEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE
- full_name TEXT
- password_hash TEXT NOT NULL (PII: sensitive — store hashed only)
- phone TEXT (PII: sensitive)
- profile_data JSONB (non-sensitive or sanitized)
- created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
- updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
- deleted_at TIMESTAMPTZ NULL -- soft-delete marker
- concurrency_token BYTEA or BIGINT or xmin (choose one consistent approach)

PII classification:
- Password and any direct identifiers (email, phone) considered sensitive. Recommend storing only hashed passwords and limit exposure of email/phone to authorized roles. Document retention and access policies in security docs.

2. Stored procedure interface conventions (signatures):
- CreateUser(IN username TEXT, IN email TEXT, IN password_hash TEXT, OUT user_id BIGINT, OUT error_code INT, OUT error_message TEXT)
  - Returns new id on success (error_code = 0)
- GetUserById(IN user_id BIGINT, OUT user_row RECORD, OUT error_code INT, OUT error_message TEXT)
  - Returns user record (except sensitive fields) or error_code = 404
- UpdateUser(IN user_id BIGINT, IN concurrency_token BYTEA, IN json_patch JSONB, OUT success BOOL, OUT error_code INT, OUT error_message TEXT)
  - Uses concurrency_token to detect conflicts; returns error_code = 409 on conflict.
- DeleteUser(IN user_id BIGINT, IN soft_delete BOOL DEFAULT TRUE, OUT success BOOL, OUT error_code INT, OUT error_message TEXT)
  - Supports soft-delete by default (sets deleted_at); hard delete allowed when soft_delete = FALSE only for authorized roles.
- ListUsers(IN filter JSONB, IN page_size INT, IN page_cursor TEXT, OUT users SETOF user_row, OUT next_cursor TEXT, OUT error_code INT, OUT error_message TEXT)
  - Supports keyset pagination via page_cursor to be more scalable than OFFSET.

3. Implementation patterns:
- Parameter validation: validate IN parameters at procedure start; return standard error_code != 0 and message. Use strict length and format checks (e.g., email regex).
- Transactions: perform multi-step operations inside an explicit transaction when invoked from CALL contexts. Use single-transaction semantics for creating/updating related tables and for audit inserts.
- Concurrency token: include a concurrency_token column (e.g., a binary/bytea hash or numeric version). UpdateUser verifies token matches current row; if mismatch, raise standardized conflict error (error_code 409).
- Soft-delete: set deleted_at = now(); filter out rows with deleted_at IS NOT NULL in ListUsers/GetUserById by default.
- Least-privilege: procedures should expose only non-sensitive fields; sensitive fields only returned to roles with explicit privileges.

4. Example CreateUser (outline)
- Validate email/username length/format.
- Begin transaction (if required by calling convention).
- INSERT INTO user_profile (...) VALUES (...) RETURNING id INTO user_id.
- Insert audit row (see audit doc).
- Commit and return error_code = 0.

5. Standardized error codes (recommended)
- 0: success
- 400: bad request / validation failed
- 401: unauthorized
- 403: forbidden
- 404: not found
- 409: concurrency conflict
- 500: internal error / logged

